{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Program Files\\\\PowerShell\\\\7\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command \"Get-Content docs/work/active/wi-1000/WI-1000-sr-035-token-scan.md; Get-Content docs/rubrics/kit-change-review.md; Get-Content project-trajectory/PROCESS.md; git diff b683375adf386c2c959abc1f806dad58973b6b2f...addb2eebca8f2ccf8c49120b183f0bf2c38dc57f -- . ':(exclude)docs/iteration' ':(exclude)docs/reviews' ':(exclude)docs/log.md' ':(exclude)docs/log.d' ':(exclude)PROJECT_STATE.html' ':(exclude)docs/open-items.html' ':(exclude)docs/stage'\"","aggregated_output":"+++\r\nid = \"WI-1000\"\r\ntitle = \"SR-035's token scan is built: a test scans the shipped registry templates and ID vocabulary for language-specific tokens\"\r\nworkstream = \"process\"\r\nsr_refs = [\"SR-035\"]\r\nspecref = \"docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.toml#LLR-171\"\r\nbuildtier = \"medium\"\r\nsafety_class = \"ordinary\"\r\nplanmode = \"none\"\r\npriority = 5\r\n+++\r\n\r\n## Context\r\n\r\nFiled by hand on 2026-11-05 (attended), routed by OI-127's ruling. TC-165 has\r\ntwo halves. Its scaffold half is driven by\r\n`tests/test_profile.py::test_every_profile_permutation_scaffolds_green`, whose\r\n`node` cases scaffold a non-Python profile and run `trace.py --strict`. That\r\ncovers integrity, but not `--strict-schema`, and the test carries no TC-165\r\ntag. The token scan of the shipped registry templates and ID vocabulary has no\r\ntest. OI-129 was ruled (a) on 2026-11-08: an approved row is built unless\r\nevidence shows substantial work, and none does.\r\n\r\nMeasured on 2026-11-08: a `--stack node` scaffold passes\r\n`trace.py --strict --strict-schema` with exit 0. The contract below finds no\r\ntoken on today's surface and exempts 37 kit-script names, 29 of them in\r\n`registries/*.template.{toml,csv}` (e.g. `trace.py`, `scripts/hats.py`). The\r\nother non-Python stacks take the same `NON_PYTHON_STACKS` branch in\r\n`bootstrap.py` as `node`, so `node` stands for them.\r\n\r\n## The scan contract (S3: closed)\r\n\r\n**Surface.** The scan reads these files:\r\n\r\n- every `project-trajectory/registries/*.template.toml` and\r\n  `*.template.csv`. That is IF-039's declared set, 15 files today. It is\r\n  globbed, so a new template joins the scan without an edit.\r\n- `project-trajectory/work/WI-000.template.md`, the work-item registry's\r\n  scaffold.\r\n- `project-trajectory/id-watermark.template`.\r\n\r\n**ID vocabulary.** The vocabulary has three declarations:\r\n\r\n- `trace.WATERMARK_SPACES`, the declared set of every id space (20 today,\r\n  pinned by `tests/test_id_watermark.py`);\r\n- `PROCESS.md` §2 \"Identifier scheme\", from its heading to the next `## `;\r\n- `INTERFACES.template.md` \"ID scheme & columns\", bounded the same way.\r\n\r\nEach off-spine prefix is also declared by its template's row ids, which the\r\nfile scan covers. If a glob matches nothing or a heading is missing, the test\r\nfails. It never skips.\r\n\r\n**Token set.** The set is declared once, as two constants in the test module:\r\n\r\n- `LANGUAGE_TOKENS`: python, pytest, pip, pyproject, conftest, unittest,\r\n  mypy, ruff, virtualenv, javascript, typescript, nodejs, npm, rust, cargo,\r\n  golang, java, kotlin, maven, gradle, junit, csharp, dotnet, nuget, ruby,\r\n  rubygems, php, cmake, powershell.\r\n- `SOURCE_EXTENSIONS`: py, pyi, ipynb, js, mjs, cjs, ts, tsx, jsx, rs, go,\r\n  java, kt, cs, rb, php, c, cc, cpp, h, hpp, swift, ps1.\r\n\r\nWords that are also ordinary English are left out: node, go, gem, composer,\r\nyarn, swift, black. Today's templates use \"node\" and \"composer\" as English.\r\n\r\n**Matching.** Matching is case-insensitive.\r\n\r\n- A word matches only between non-`[A-Za-z0-9_]` boundaries.\r\n- An extension matches as a file-name suffix: a name character before the\r\n  dot and none after it.\r\n- One exemption applies: a `.py` match that is exactly `<name>.py` or\r\n  `scripts/<name>.py`, where `project-trajectory/scripts/<name>.py` exists. A\r\n  kit script is the kit's own toolchain, which every adopter runs whatever\r\n  its stack. Naming it names the delivered checks that SR-035 presupposes, not\r\n  the adopter's language. `src/check.py` and `tests/*.py` are still reported.\r\n\r\nA hit is reported as file, line and token.\r\n\r\n**Outside the claim.**\r\n\r\n- Any token not in the two constants.\r\n- All other kit prose. This includes the rest of `PROCESS.md`, which carries\r\n  marked Python reference examples by design.\r\n- `registries/last-approved-README.template.md` and `specs/`, which are not\r\n  registry templates.\r\n- Restatements of the scheme, such as `KICKOFF_PROMPT.md` and\r\n  `AGENTS.template.md`.\r\n- The kit's scripts.\r\n\r\nThe test does not claim to detect arbitrary language-specific free text.\r\n\r\n## Done-when\r\n\r\n- [ ] A new in-process module, `tests/test_language_neutral_scheme.py`\r\n      (tagged `Implements: TC-165`), declares the two constants and one scan\r\n      function over the surface above. The shipped surface scans clean.\r\n- [ ] Planted cases run against a temp copy of the surface:\r\n      - `pytest` and `src/app.py` planted into a template copy are each\r\n        reported with file and line;\r\n      - `scripts/trace.py` is not reported;\r\n      - `src/check.py` is reported;\r\n      - a removed §2 heading fails the test.\r\n- [ ] `test_every_profile_permutation_scaffolds_green` runs\r\n      `trace.py --strict --strict-schema` (`--strict` includes the integrity\r\n      tier) and carries the node-level tag `Implements: TC-165`.\r\n- [ ] TC-165's `evidence` reads\r\n      `tests/test_profile.py::test_every_profile_permutation_scaffolds_green; tests/test_language_neutral_scheme.py`,\r\n      and `check_trajectory --strict` finds tags and evidence equal.\r\n- [ ] No shipped file changes, so no RESYNC entry is owed.\r\n- [ ] Review bar: A+B (REVIEW-A plus an independent REVIEW-B).\r\n\r\n## Architectural delta\r\n\r\nThe row adds no mode class, no adopter-facing value domain and no boundary.\r\n\r\n- The new output is a test result. It depends on the template bytes IF-039\r\n  carries, on `trace.WATERMARK_SPACES`, on the two declared doc sections and\r\n  on the token constants.\r\n- This stays inside LLR-171 and CMP-009.\r\n- The scaffold half changes one flag in an existing test.\r\n- No IF row, component or flow changes.\r\n\r\n## Critique dispositions\r\n\r\nRound 1 (fresh Codex session, 2026-11-08): CHANGES-REQUESTED, one MAJOR and\r\ntwo MINORs.\r\n\r\n- **S3, the finite scan contract: adopted.** \"The scan contract (S3: closed)\"\r\n  names the surface (\"every `project-trajectory/registries/*.template.toml`\r\n  and `*.template.csv` ...\"), the ID vocabulary (`trace.WATERMARK_SPACES`,\r\n  `PROCESS.md` §2, `INTERFACES.template.md` \"ID scheme & columns\"), the token\r\n  set \"declared once, as two constants in the test module\", the matching\r\n  rule, and what is outside the claim: \"The test does not claim to detect\r\n  arbitrary language-specific free text.\"\r\n- **S4, the planning ruling: adopted as `planmode = \"none\"`.** With the\r\n  contract closed, the Done-when fully specifies the diff:\r\n  - one file-scan test with its planted cases;\r\n  - one flag and one tag in an existing test;\r\n  - one evidence cell.\r\n\r\n  That is S4's cheap-test exception.\r\n- **S5, the architectural delta: adopted.** \"Architectural delta\" states:\r\n  \"The row adds no mode class, no adopter-facing value domain and no\r\n  boundary.\"\r\n\r\nRound 2 (fresh Codex session, confirming, 2026-11-08): APPROVE, no findings;\r\nevery adopted disposition confirmed.\r\n# Rubric — Reviewing a change to this kit (WI-852)\r\n\r\n**Adjudicates:** the checks a review of a change to this repository owes beyond\r\nthe kit's reviewer brief, which already asks for the failure classes, the\r\nDone-when coverage map, the red-before test, the unrepresentable clause and the\r\nrequirement-row sweep.\r\n**Used by:** an independent reviewer of a lane, when its brief is rendered with\r\n`review_brief.py review --rubric docs/rubrics/kit-change-review.md`.\r\n\r\n## Intent\r\n\r\nThe rules this repository holds its own lanes to (`CLAUDE.md`, and the owner\r\nrulings the spine-authoring skill records) that the kit's shipped brief cannot\r\nname, because an adopter's repository has its own.\r\n\r\n## Anchors\r\n\r\n**K1 — Requirement text is true, and the right way round.** Read every added or\r\namended SN, SR, LLR and TC cell against PROCESS.md and the code. *Bad:* a row\r\nstating the inverse of the rule its code implements; earlier waves shipped\r\nthat, and code review missed it.\r\n\r\n**K2 — Owner ruling R2, as review-churn ruling 5 refines it.** No SN or SR\r\ncell names an implementation carrier (a script, command, file or function),\r\nacceptance included. Current-carrier evidence lives in the TC rows that verify\r\nthe row, and a shipped boundary artifact (a registry, template, config file or\r\nlauncher) may be named at the LLR and TC tiers only. An approved cell keeps its\r\napproved text, names included, until it is amended through its authority.\r\n\r\n**K3 — Back-links and approved rows.** Each `Implements:` tag names the LLR\r\nwhose `module` is that file, and the function appears in that LLR's\r\n`code_symbol`. An approved row changes only where the spec grants it, and no\r\nStatus flips.\r\n\r\n**B1 — A finding stated as a guess.** Report a finding as a claim confirmed by\r\nreading or running the code, never as a guess.\r\n# Development Process (template)\r\n\r\n<!-- kit-only -->\r\nCopied into a new repo as `docs/process.md` by `scripts/bootstrap.py`, which\r\nomits any sections `docs/kit-profile` turns off — § labels never renumber; an\r\nomitted section keeps its heading plus a one-line stub.\r\n<!-- /kit-only -->\r\nCanonical method for a gated, requirement-traced project. It is\r\n**stack-agnostic** — wire the harness commands to your project's\r\nlanguage/tooling — but it **requires a git repository**: diffable registries,\r\nthe append-only log, reviewed Status-change commits, and gate approval\r\nall presume git. That substrate was always assumed; it is named here so no\r\none designs around its absence. Other docs reference this file by section rather than\r\nrestating it. Its terms are defined once in [`glossary.md`](glossary.md),\r\nincluding a lane's lifecycle: its states, substates and conditions\r\n([lane state](glossary.md#lane-state)). Links are authored for the scaffolded home (`docs/process.md`\r\nbeside `docs/process-options.md`); at the kit's own location some relative\r\nlinks don't resolve.\r\n\r\n**Read this file top to bottom for the load-bearing core** (§1–§7): roles, ids,\r\nthe §3 discipline, the gates, the verdict protocol, the harness contract. The\r\nopt-in layers — phased delivery, lifecycle tags, cross-project interfaces (§8),\r\nNFR/perf budgets (§9), the multi-repo scale ladder (§10), and the §7 boundary\r\nnotes — are summarized here with an **applies-when** and expanded in\r\n[`process-options.md`](process-options.md); skip any that your scope doesn't hit.\r\n\r\n**Minimum profile — a standalone one-module project needs exactly:** the five\r\nspine hats (§1), the id scheme (§2), the §3 traceability/anti-duplication\r\ndiscipline, the bars **DevStg-Reqs→DevStg-Tests→DevStg-Impl** + the owner's final read (§4), the verdict protocol (§5),\r\nreview triage (§6), and the harness (§7). Everything else is opt-in: skip §8,\r\n§9, §10, the `Phase`/`Lifecycle` tags, and every \"optional\" tripwire until the\r\nscope forces it. (Skipping the `Lifecycle` *tag* doesn't skip DevStg-Reqs's per-phase\r\nedge-case sweep — an explicit n/a per phase is all the bookkeeping this rung\r\nowes.) That default is rung 1 of the §10 ladder.\r\n\r\n**Proportionality — the process right-sizes itself.** Text-representable,\r\nchange-trackable artifacts are the **ideal** this process reaches for, not an\r\nentry requirement: track *about* an asset in text even where the asset itself\r\ncan't be diffed. Where verification can't be mechanized, a **recorded human\r\nattestation** (§4 `Attest`) is the honest floor — trust-based by nature (the box\r\ncan be checked without the work having happened), so the process's job is to make\r\nit **explicit, named, and auditable**, never to pass it off as a mechanized\r\ncheck. Over-aggressive traceability is itself a failure mode: right-sizing is the\r\nprocess working, not a compromise of it (see §3 \"Right-sizing\"). The same\r\ncalibration sets the **decision-surfacing dial** at project setup — how often the\r\ndriver pauses for a human decision (§6). Full doctrine — including the\r\ncreative/subjective stance and the dial — in\r\n[`process-options.md`](process-options.md#proportionality-doctrine).\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 1. Roles (hats), not necessarily separate agents\r\n\r\nOne driver wears these hats in sequence, keeping context. Spawn a *separate*\r\nagent for an independent pre-gate review, to step a mechanical subtask down a\r\ntier, or to give bulk content a dedicated context (all §6) — never to split the\r\nhats' shared context.\r\n\r\n| Hat | Owns (single source of truth) |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| Stakeholder | `requirements/stakeholder-needs.toml` (SN-###), failure-mode expectations included |\r\n| UX / Docs | documentation quality, quick-reference, usability findings |\r\n| System Engineer | `requirements/system-requirements.toml` (SR-###); **gatekeeper** |\r\n| Software Engineer | `requirements/low-level-requirements.toml` (LLR-###) + code + `runtime-flows.md` |\r\n| Test Engineer | `test/test-cases.toml` (TC-###) + the check harness + coverage/trace reports |\r\n\r\nA hat only edits artifacts it owns; to 
